The deeper move is that you can sketch a relationship before you have the tools to fully express it. A phrase, a diagram, or a half-built scene can preserve enough structure for a future model to develop it in several domains. Each domain can reveal something the original sketch left implicit.

Take your idea that different routes through a math problem could be different characters’ journeys. In algebra, the routes are derivations you can check step by step. In storytelling, the choice of route reveals character: one person takes a shortcut that works only under a hidden assumption, while another takes the long way and finds where the shortcut breaks. In physics, the same structure prompts a different question: if two processes reach the same measured endpoint, what traces could tell you which path was taken? Bring that question back to the story and you get a new scene: two characters arrive at the same door, and only one is carrying evidence of what happened along the way.

That’s what the copy-back loop can do for Pantheon. You save the original sketch, then keep the discoveries that came from moving it between domains. A future model inherits both what you saw before you could fully explain it and the trail of what it became. It can follow that trail further or return to the sketch and see a possibility we missed.